The Conceptual Mapping FrameworkTo manage this, we use a Four-Layer Interaction Model:Actor Identity: Defines the persona (e.g., Referee, System Monitor).Responsibility Scope: The "RACI" definition (e.g., who is Accountable for the integrity of the score? The Referee).Data Boundaries (Write/Read/Execute): The strict technical limitations (e.g., the Broadcaster can Read the score but cannot Execute a change to it).Interaction Trigger: What event causes the actor to act (e.g., the System Monitor acts when a "Heartbeat" fails).Ecosystem Role Overview (Abstract)This grid provides the high-level mapping of your Tennis Suite ecosystem before we begin the deep-dive iteration for each persona.PersonaRole TypeData InteractionPrimary BoundaryTournament HostAdministrativeCreate/Manage/AssignBusiness & Infrastructure RulesRefereeActive (Authoritative)Create/Write (Ground Truth)Match Scoring & Laws of TennisCourt MarshalActive (Logistical)Update/WriteCourt Status & Player FlowSystem MonitorProactive (Technical)Monitor/Validate/RepairNetwork & System HealthBroadcasterReactive (Observer)Consume/VisualizeMedia Feed & OverlaysTournament DelegateAdministrative (God-mode)Override/Audit/ApproveSystem-wide IntegrityPlayerPassive/ActiveView/ConfirmPersonal Match ProgressPublic FanPassiveView OnlyCinematic BroadcastPrinciples to CheckBeyond the RACI matrix, there are three critical architectural principles we must apply to verify this system:Principle of Least Privilege: Does each role have only the minimum access necessary to perform its job? (e.g., Does the Broadcaster really need access to the registration CSV? No).Separation of Concerns: Have we ensured that the people managing the infrastructure (Monitor) are not the same people managing the creative content (Broadcaster)? This prevents cognitive overload and technical failure.Auditability & Immutability: Does every "Write" action by an actor leave a permanent, time-stamped record (Audit Trail) that cannot be altered?